---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro'
import { site } from '@/data/site'
---

<Base
  title="Notch privacy — local by default"
  description="What Notch stores on your PC, how the camera and Spotify tokens are handled, and what this website collects."
>
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="page-head">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Privacy</span>
      <h1>Local by default.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Notch has no backend. There is no account, no telemetry endpoint, and no server that could
        receive your data.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What stays on your PC</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>File</th><th>Contents</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>%APPDATA%\notch\notch-state.json</code></td>
            <td>Settings, tasks, timer state and posture totals.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>%APPDATA%\notch\spotify-auth.json</code></td>
            <td>Spotify access and refresh tokens. PKCE, so no client secret exists.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>%APPDATA%\notch\notch.log</code></td>
            <td>Startup and diagnostic lines.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Your notes folder</td>
            <td>Plain Markdown files, edited in place.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>The camera</h2>
      <p>
        Frames are captured in the app process, scored, and discarded. Nothing is written to disk,
        buffered for later, or sent anywhere. The Windows camera indicator stays lit while the camera
        is in use, and posture is off until you enable it.
      </p>

      <h2>Spotify</h2>
      <p>
        Desktop control uses the Windows system media session and needs no account. The optional full
        connection uses OAuth PKCE, so no client secret exists. Tokens are stored locally and can be
        removed with <strong>Disconnect</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>AI sessions</h2>
      <p>
        Notch reads the local session files those tools already write. Sending a message starts the
        tool’s own CLI process; content goes to that provider under their terms, not through Notch.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        Cookieless analytics: page views and download events, with no identifiers and no cross-site
        tracking. Downloads are served by GitHub, whose own privacy terms apply.
      </p>

      <h2>Buying Notch</h2>
      <p>
        Purchases go through a third-party checkout provider. They process your payment details
        under their own privacy policy — we never see your card number. We receive only the email
        address used for the purchase, so we can deliver the license and handle refunds. The app
        itself has no account and no license server to contact.
      </p>

      <h2>Deleting everything</h2>
      <p>
        Uninstall the app, then delete <code>%APPDATA%\notch</code>. Your notes remain until you
        delete them yourself. Questions go to <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
